gemm_tile_sweep_c_single_buffer_npu.log -- the GEMM tile sweep that single-buffering the C
output tile makes possible: m/k/n tiles of the 4x4 whole_array.py design in bf16 and int8 at
2048^3, the surviving tiles across three more shapes, and a same-sitting CPU bf16 baseline.

Desktop 2: Ryzen 7 8700G (8 Zen 4 cores), Phoenix XDNA1, Windows 11; mlir-aie v1.4.2 ironenv
(Peano llvm-aie 22), XRT 2.21.0, NPU driver 32.0.20101.3760, power mode Default (1.80 GHz,
results/aie/clock_probe_npu.log). Clean sitting 2026-09-07 23:59:26 to 2026-09-08 00:04:48.
An earlier screen of the same tiles (18:33-19:1x, appendix) ran under a 104-layer AdaRound
job and is superseded by this run.

WHY
---
docs/SILICON.md 3.1 predicts each tile's input-bound ceiling from bytes per MAC streamed into
the core: the default 64x32 tile at 67 % of bf16 peak, 64x64 at 100 %, 128x32 and 32x128 equal
at 80 %. bf16 could never reach 64x64 because its double-buffered f32 C tile alone is 32 KB of
the 64 KB L1 (results/aie/int8_matmul_sweep_npu.log measured the miss at exactly the 3,328 B
stack). Objective K2 asked what the 16 KB a single-buffered C tile frees actually buys, and
whether the B/MAC model's predictions hold once more tiles are reachable.

PATCH
-----
kernels/gemm_tile_sweep/whole_array_c_single_buffer.patch against mlir-aie v1.4.2's
programming_examples/basic/matrix_multiplication/whole_array/whole_array.py: a --c-single-buffer
{0,1} flag that sets the per-core C_L1L2 ObjectFIFO depth to 1 instead of the design's
fifo_depth (2). Nothing else changes; A and B stay double-buffered. L1 per core then holds
2A + 2B + 1C + a 3,328 B stack, with A = m*k*2 B, B = k*n*2 B, C = m*n*4 B (f32 accumulator)
in bf16, half that for A and B in int8 (i32 C). kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py
prints that estimate ("L1 est") on every run so a compile failure can be read against it.

WHAT WAS RUN
------------
run_clean.sh (verbatim below): PART 1 the 2048^3 screen, ten bf16 tiles; PART 1b three of
them with --b-col-maj 1; PART 2 four tiles across 1024^3, 4096x2048x2048, 2048x4096x4096;
PART 3 three int8 tiles at 2048^3; PART 4 the CPU bf16/fp32 baseline (torch, resnet_env, 8
threads) in the same sitting. Every NPU run: --iters 10 --warmup 3, GFLOPS = 2MKN over the
NPU-bracket average (whole_array.py's own "NPU time"), 4 columns, the compile cache warm from
the screen. Contention control: the script snapshots other python/compiler processes at the
start, after the NPU part and at the end (all three empty, see raw output); a 1 s --json
instance of tools/hwinfo_npu_bridge.exe watched the device throughout and listed only the
sweep's own three contexts (pids 29544, 21304, 10928, 88 MB each, one at a time); the user's
own bridge instance (0.5 s, xrt-smi reads only, no hardware context) was running as always.
Another session's 128-stream classifier sweep had ended at 23:57:43 and did not resume.

--- run_clean.sh ---
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Clean re-run (no concurrent AdaRound): the full 2048^3 tile screen again, then the three
# best bf16 tiles and the default across the K2 shapes, then int8 at its two new tiles,
# then the CPU bf16 baseline in resnet_env -- one sitting.
set -u
REPO="$HOME/PycharmProjects/ryzen-ai-xdna1-quantization"
export VIRTUAL_ENV='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv'
export PATH="C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Scripts:C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie\bin:C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie\lib:C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\lib:C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt:C:\Windows\System32\AMD:$PATH"
export MLIR_AIE_INSTALL_DIR='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\mlir_aie'
export PEANO_INSTALL_DIR='C:\Users\<user>\mlir-aie\ironenv\Lib\site-packages\llvm-aie'
export XRT_ROOT='C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt'
export PYTHONPATH='C:\Xilinx\XRT\xrt_sdk\xrt\python'
export NPU2='0'
unset XILINX_XRT
cd "$REPO" || exit 1
snap() { powershell -NoProfile -Command "Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Where-Object { \$_.Name -match '^(python|aiecc|clang|peano)' } | ForEach-Object { '{0} {1} {2}' -f \$_.ProcessId, \$_.Name, (\$_.CommandLine -replace '\s+',' ').Substring(0, [Math]::Min(140, (\$_.CommandLine).Length)) }"; }
D="python kernels/int8_matmul_sweep/npu_matmul_sweep.py --iters 10 --warmup 3"
S=2048x2048x2048
SH="1024x1024x1024,4096x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096"
echo "=== K2 tile sweep, clean sitting. start $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') ==="
echo "--- other python/compiler processes at start (contention check) ---"; snap; echo "--- end ---"
echo; echo "######## PART 1: the 2048^3 screen, repeated with the machine otherwise idle ########"
echo; echo "######## S1: bf16 64/64/32 double-C (default) ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 0 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "######## S2,S4: bf16 64/64/32 and 128/64/32 single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S,$S@128"
echo; echo "######## S3,S9: bf16 64/64/64 and 128/64/64 (predicted OVER) single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S,$S@128"
echo; echo "######## S5: bf16 32/64/128 single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 128 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S@32"
echo; echo "######## S6: bf16 64/128/32 single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 128 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "######## S10: bf16 64/128/64 (predicted OVER) single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 128 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "######## S7: bf16 128/32/64 single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 32 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S@128"
echo; echo "######## S8: bf16 64/32/128 single-C ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 32 -n 128 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "######## PART 1b: is the m-vs-n asymmetry B's DMA burst length? --b-col-maj 1 makes B's contiguous run k instead of n ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 1 --b-col-maj 1 --shapes "$S@128"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 128 --c-single-buffer 1 --b-col-maj 1 --shapes "$S@32"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --b-col-maj 1 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "######## PART 2: shapes x tiles (bf16) ########"
echo; echo "######## default 64/64/32 double-C across shapes ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 0 --shapes "$SH"
echo; echo "######## 64/64/64 single-C across shapes ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$SH"
echo; echo "######## 32/64/128 single-C across shapes ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 128 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "1024x1024x1024@32,4096x2048x2048@32,2048x4096x4096@32"
echo; echo "######## 128/64/32 single-C across shapes ########"
$D --dtype bf16 -k 64 -n 32 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "1024x1024x1024@128,4096x2048x2048@128,2048x4096x4096@128"
echo; echo "######## PART 3: int8 at 2048^3 -- 64/64/64 double-C (reference), 128/64/64 and 64/128/64 single-C ########"
$D --dtype i8 -k 64 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 0 --shapes "$S"
$D --dtype i8 -k 64 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S@128"
$D --dtype i8 -k 128 -n 64 --c-single-buffer 1 --shapes "$S"
echo; echo "--- other python/compiler processes after NPU part ---"; snap; echo "--- end ---"
echo; echo "######## PART 4: CPU bf16/fp32 baseline, resnet_env, same sitting ########"
unset OMP_NUM_THREADS MKL_NUM_THREADS TORCH_NUM_THREADS
eval "$(conda shell.bash hook)" && conda activate resnet_env && PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python kernels/bf16_matmul_sweep/cpu_matmul_sweep.py --iters 20 --warmup 5 --shapes "1024x1024x1024,2048x2048x2048,4096x2048x2048,2048x4096x4096"
echo; echo "--- other python/compiler processes at end ---"; snap; echo "--- end ---"
echo "=== done $(date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S') ==="


