FINDING
-------
1. The L1 model is exact. 2A + 2B + (1|2)C + 3,328 B decides every compile: 25 of the 26
   predicted-fit tiles compiled (the 26th hit the C-output stride cap of 2.6, not L1), and
   both predicted-over tiles (128/64/64 and 64/128/64 in bf16, 85,248 B) died in the allocator.
   So the 128x64 / 64x128 tiles are out of reach for bf16 even single-buffered -- MEASURED
   now, where docs/SILICON.md K2 had it as derived.

2. Single-buffering C alone costs 6.5 % at the default tile (1715.59 -> 1603.26 GFLOPS): the
   overlap it removes is real. What it buys is the tiles it lets in: 64/64/64 at 2501.71
   GFLOPS, 1.46x the default, 33.9 % of the 7.37 TFLOPS peak -- and 32/64/128 at 2494.61,
   the same within 0.3 %. Those two are the best bf16 tiles the CLI can reach, and they
   track each other at every shape (Table 4); the best bf16 number in this repo is now
   2700.44 GFLOPS at 2048x4096x4096 (32/64/128; 64/64/64 reads 2653.05 there), 36.6 % of peak,
   1.89x the CPU's same-sitting bf16 mean and 1.53x its best-case min.

3. The 3.1 B/MAC model is incomplete in two named ways.
   (a) 128x32 and 32x128 have identical B/MAC (0.0781, 80 %) and measure 2494.61 against
       2136.09 -- 1.17x apart. Table 3 says why: with B column-major, so that both tiles
       stream B in k=64-element runs instead of n-element runs, 128/64/32 gains 4.3 %,
       32/64/128 loses 12.6 %, 64/64/64 moves -2.9 %, and the order of the first two
       flips. B's contiguous DMA run length (n elements, row-major) is a term the
       bytes-per-MAC count does not have.
   (b) k is a term too. 128/32/64 and 64/32/128 have better B/MAC (0.0469, "100 %") than
       64/64/64 and measure 2070.87 and 2146.76 -- below it; 64/128/32 has the default tile's
       B/MAC and measures 1809.65, 5.5 % above the default. Halving k halves the MACs each
       A/B tile handshake amortises; doubling it helps at the same B/MAC.
   The model still orders the tiles that differ only in B/MAC correctly and puts the
   ceiling in the right place; at 64x64 the measured 33.9 % of peak against a 100 %
   input-bound ceiling says the remaining two thirds are not input bandwidth.

4. int8 gains from the freed L1 too, and more than the contaminated screen showed:
   128/64/64 4683.89 GOPS (1.091x) and 64/128/64 4852.06 (1.130x) against 64/64/64 double-buffered
   4293.63. The k=128 tile is the better use of the 16 KB in int8, consistent with 3(b).

5. The contaminated screen (appendix) had every control 6-11 % low and the int8 gains at
   +4 %; the tile ranking it gave was right, the sizes were not. This run supersedes it.

NOT TESTED
----------
- Any tile past the 64 KB L1 (128x64, 64x128 and up): needs a design change (C through the
  mem tile, or an accumulator that is not a full f32 tile), not a flag.
- m=128 tiles at N=4096: the C-output stride cap (2.6) fails the compile; a shim-BD layout
  problem, unrelated to L1, not pursued.
- 32x64 and 32x32 tiles, int8 across shapes, --b-col-maj at shapes other than 2048^3, and
  more than 10 iterations per point (the 2048^3 64/64/64 point agrees with
  bf16_matmul_n64_single_buffer_npu.log's 2477.23 within 1 %, so the sitting is typical).
- Whether 32/64/128 keeps its lead over 64/64/64 with M < 1024 (m=32 forces M % 256 == 0
  and puts more, smaller row-tiles on the array; at 1024^3 it is behind).

